PHASE 5 - VALIDATE ALL PATTERNS (prove each rule covers what it claims)

For every rule produce tests/fixtures/<ID>/PATTERNS.md and the fixtures for it:
  1. Pattern matrix. List the variants the rule must handle along these axes,
     one fixture per row (combine where sensible):
       construct form   e.g. for-range vs for-in-list, with vs with new,
                        case vs if/elsif, nested vs flat, named vs positional
                        arguments
       context          package level, method, nested method, declare block,
                        loop body, loop header, with block, exception handler
       source type      SCRIPT PACKAGE, DDIC VALID, REP DTM/layout, RULE TEMPL
                        expression, MSG IN/OUT (only those the tool processes)
       noise            match inside a comment, inside a string, suppressed by
                        --@NoCheck, inside --#ifdef, postp_start constants,
                        upper/mixed case, odd whitespace and line breaks
       boundary         exactly at the threshold, one below, one above
     Mark each row pos/neg/edge and name the fixture file that covers it.
  2. Corpus census. tools/pattern_census.py: for each rule's anchor node
     type, count the distinct ancestor chains it appears in across the 500
     sources (e.g. "method>stmts>for_stmt>stmts>if_stmt"). Report chains
     covering 95% of occurrences and flag any chain with NO fixture. Add
     fixtures until the 95% is covered.
  3. Branch coverage. Run coverage (dev-only) on fixtures per rule; every
     branch in rules/<id>.py must be hit. List uncovered lines and add
     fixtures for them.
  4. Mutation check. tools/mutate.py: for each neg_ fixture, apply the rule's
     declared "violation edit" (e.g. remove the else branch, copy the lookup
     into the loop) and assert the rule now fires. A rule that never fires
     on a mutated file has a dead check.
  5. Precision sampling. tools/sample_review.py <ID> --n 20: picks 20 random
     findings of that rule from the corpus run into reviews/<ID>.csv with a
     blank verdict column (TP/FP). I label them. Report precision per rule;
     a rule under 80% gets severity info until fixed. Every FP I label
     becomes a neg_ fixture.
  6. Parse coverage. Report % of corpus sources that parse. Rules can't
     validate what the parser can't read, so list the top unparsed
     constructs as a separate backlog.
STOP. Report per rule: pattern rows, census coverage %, branch coverage %,
mutation result, precision (if labelled).

